  "bounded_own_incremental_examples": [
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "After we import pygame it is a good idea to initialize it, like so:",
      "raw_code_characters": 27,
      "raw_code_sha256": "06fd3511a450209fc9b90a1f8ce300326ce053c731908345f05412ae8e24648b",
      "owned_api_tokens": []
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "Once we've done that we need to setup a windows that will represent our game.",
      "raw_code_characters": 124,
      "raw_code_sha256": "89605399055792c00ba2889289c8273b10461370b0003c93a4013dbf110c2a0e",
      "owned_api_tokens": []
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "This is great but we'd like to give our game a more creative name than \"pygame window\". To do this we can can type the following.",
      "raw_code_characters": 40,
      "raw_code_sha256": "b64d8e404eeeb48169b887021b339d19a294d377e9d204d2db18853c6390dee6",
      "owned_api_tokens": []
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "Inside the loop we will implement a time delay so we can control the speed of the game. We will also start by checking for some specific events.",
      "raw_code_characters": 640,
      "raw_code_sha256": "0ea228fef807dc865a2b25b98c6358da07696840cbdf50563eb5aa422fc68fb8",
      "owned_api_tokens": [
        "pygame.event.get"
      ]
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "Now we can draw a rectangle to the screen to represent our character. We will draw the rectangle in the main loop so that it is constantly redrawn each time we loop.",
      "raw_code_characters": 512,
      "raw_code_sha256": "7bc3d68091f8563eea7de94dc69bde80f6a6057a0423c125fee86d49cdb1084b",
      "owned_api_tokens": [
        "pygame.event.get"
      ]
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "Inside the if statements we will change the value of the variables x and y to move the character. This brings me to the coordinate system. In pygame the top left corner of the screen is (0,0) and the bottom right is (width, height). This means to move up we subtract from the y of our character and to move down we add to the y.",
      "raw_code_characters": 643,
      "raw_code_sha256": "017968736cf7227325b5b45674ef1bb93fb68067329f94b6b4ddadd2f79cce4a",
      "owned_api_tokens": [
        "pygame.event.get",
        "pygame.key.get_pressed",
        "pygame.K_LEFT",
        "pygame.K_RIGHT",
        "pygame.K_UP",
        "pygame.K_DOWN"
      ]
    },
    {
      "preceding_own_explanation": "To fix this we simply have to draw over the previous shape before redrawing another one. We can use window.fill(color) to do this.",
